left:4px solid #e29c28;background:#fff9ea;padding:15px 19px;border-radius:9px}.step{border-left:3px solid #b8d5f8;padding-left:16px;margin:18px 0}.footer{color:#687891;text-align:center;padding:36px 15px}.toc a{color:#145eac;text-decoration:none}.toc a:hover{text-decoration:underline}@media print{.topbar,.no-print{display:none!important}.section{break-inside:avoid;box-shadow:none}.hero{color:#fff!important;print-color-adjust:exact}}</style></head><body>
<nav class="navbar navbar-expand-lg navbar-dark topbar"><div class="container-fluid px-3"><a class="navbar-brand" href="/">Commercial IT Bootcamp</a><button class="navbar-toggler" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#bootcampNav" aria-label="Toggle navigation"><span class="navbar-toggler-icon"></span></button><div class="collapse navbar-collapse" id="bootcampNav"><ul class="navbar-nav me-auto"><li class="nav-item"><a class="nav-link" href="/">Home</a></li><li class="nav-item dropdown"><a class="nav-link dropdown-toggle" href="#" role="button" data-bs-toggle="dropdown">Training</a><ul class="dropdown-menu"><li><a class="dropdown-item" href="/training">Training Home</a></li></ul></li><li class="nav-item dropdown"><a class="nav-link dropdown-toggle" href="#" role="button" data-bs-toggle="dropdown">E-Commerce Reference</a><ul class="dropdown-menu"><li><a class="dropdown-item" href="/ecommerce">Overview</a></li></ul></li><li class="nav-item dropdown"><a class="nav-link dropdown-toggle active" href="#" role="button" data-bs-toggle="dropdown">Internships</a><ul class="dropdown-menu"><li><a class="dropdown-item" href="/internships/cloud-computing">Cloud Computing</a></li></ul></li><li class="nav-item"><a class="nav-link" href="/about">About</a></li></ul></div></div></nav>
<main class="container" style="max-width:1290px"><div class="py-4"><a href="/internships/cloud-computing" class="text-decoration-none fw-semibold">← Cloud Computing</a></div><header class="hero"><div class="eyebrow">Amazon Web Services · 02 · VPC, Networking & Connectivity</div><h1 class="mt-3">Amazon VPC &amp; CIDR</h1><p>Design a secure, highly available AWS network using IPv4 CIDR allocation, public and private subnets, route tables, gateways, security groups, and a multi-Availability-Zone deployment model.</p><div><span class="glass">Foundational networking</span><span class="glass">2 Availability Zones</span><span class="glass">3-tier topology</span><span class="glass">Hands-on lab</span></div></header>
<section class="section toc"><h2><i class="bi bi-compass me-2"></i>Learning path</h2><div class="row g-2"><div class="col-md-4"><a href="#architecture">01 · Reference architecture</a></div><div class="col-md-4"><a href="#fundamentals">02 · VPC & CIDR basics</a></div><div class="col-md-4"><a href="#addressing">03 · Address plan</a></div><div class="col-md-4"><a href="#routing">04 · Route tables & gateways</a></div><div class="col-md-4"><a href="#security">05 · Security boundaries</a></div><div class="col-md-4"><a href="#lab">06 · AWS build exercise</a></div></div></section>
<section class="section" id="architecture"><h2><i class="bi bi-diagram-3 me-2"></i>01 · End-to-end reference architecture</h2><p>This design spans two Availability Zones. Public subnets provide NAT Gateway placement, a public Application Load Balancer distributes HTTPS traffic to private application targets, and isolated database subnets host database resources. Each subnet has a unique, non-overlapping CIDR range within the VPC.</p><div class="diagram-frame"><img class="architecture" src="/images/architecture/aws-vpc-cidr-architecture.svg" alt="AWS VPC with two Availability Zones, six subnets, Internet Gateway, load balancer, NAT Gateways, application instances and database tier"></div><div class="d-flex flex-wrap gap-2 mt-3 no-print"><a class="btn btn-primary" href="/images/architecture/aws-vpc-cidr-architecture.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><i class="bi bi-arrows-fullscreen me-1"></i>Open scalable diagram</a><a class="btn btn-outline-primary" href="/images/architecture/aws-vpc-cidr-architecture.png" download>Download high-resolution PNG</a></div><div class="note mt-3"><strong>Diagram convention:</strong> Arrows express the conceptual request path, not every VPC route hop. An internet-facing ALB is deployed to public subnets in both AZs; the illustration summarizes its shared regional entry point. NAT Gateways provide outbound connections for private instances, not inbound Internet access.</div></section>
<section class="section" id="fundamentals"><h2><i class="bi bi-globe2 me-2"></i>02 · What is a VPC? What does CIDR mean?</h2><p><strong>Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)</strong> is a logically isolated network in AWS where you control IP address spaces, subnets, routing and network security. <strong>Classless Inter-Domain Routing (CIDR)</strong> describes a contiguous block of IP addresses using a network prefix: <code>10.20.0.0/16</code> means the first 16 bits identify the IPv4 network and the remaining 16 bits define the address space.</p><div class="row g-3"><div class="col-md-4"><div class="metric"><strong>/16</strong>65,536 total IPv4 addresses<br><small>Common VPC example: 10.20.0.0/16</small></div></div><div class="col-md-4"><div class="metric"><strong>/24</strong>256 total IPv4 addresses<br><small>AWS subnet: 251 usable IPv4 addresses</small></div></div><div class="col-md-4"><div class="metric"><strong>5 reserved</strong>AWS reserves the first four and last IPv4 address in each subnet.</div></div></div><h3>Address math</h3><p>Total IPv4 addresses = <strong>2<sup>(32 − prefix)</sup></strong>. For <code>/24</code>, 2<sup>8</sup> = 256. AWS subnet usable IPv4 addresses = total − 5. A subnet belongs to exactly one Availability Zone and its CIDR block must not overlap another subnet in the same VPC.</p></section>
<section class="section" id="addressing"><h2><i class="bi bi-table me-2"></i>03 · Six-subnet IPv4 address plan</h2><div class="table-responsive"><table class="table table-bordered table-striped align-middle"><thead><tr><th>Tier</th><th>AZ A</th><th>AZ B</th><th>Typical workload</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Public</td><td><code>10.20.0.0/24</code></td><td><code>10.20.1.0/24</code></td><td>ALB networking, NAT Gateway</td></tr><tr><td>Private application</td><td><code>10.20.10.0/24</code></td><td><code>10.20.11.0/24</code></td><td>EC2 / ECS workloads</td></tr><tr><td>Isolated database</td><td><code>10.20.20.0/24</code></td><td><code>10.20.21.0/24</code></td><td>RDS subnet group / Multi-AZ deployment</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>All six ranges fit inside <code>10.20.0.0/16</code>. Each <code>/24</code> subnet offers 251 AWS-usable IPv4 addresses. Plan additional headroom for load balancing, scaling, managed-service allocation and future growth.</p></section>
<section class="section" id="routing"><h2><i class="bi bi-signpost-split me-2"></i>04 · Routing, Internet Gateway and NAT Gateway</h2><div class="table-responsive"><table class="table table-bordered align-middle"><thead><tr><th>Route table</th><th>Destination</th><th>Target</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>All subnet route tables</td><td><code>10.20.0.0/16</code></td><td><code>local</code></td><td>Intra-VPC communication subject to security policy</td></tr><tr><td>Public A/B</td><td><code>0.0.0.0/0</code></td><td>Internet Gateway</td><td>Public Internet routing</td></tr><tr><td>Private app A</td><td><code>0.0.0.0/0</code></td><td>NAT Gateway A</td><td>AZ-local IPv4 egress</td></tr><tr><td>Private app B</td><td><code>0.0.0.0/0</code></td><td>NAT Gateway B</td><td>AZ-local IPv4 egress</td></tr><tr><td>Isolated DB A/B</td><td>No default Internet route</td><td>Local VPC routes only</td><td>Keep databases isolated from Internet routes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>An Internet Gateway provides a route to/from the Internet for properly addressed and permitted resources. A public subnet is characterized by a default route to an Internet Gateway; a public IP and appropriate security rules are also needed for direct IPv4 Internet connectivity. A NAT Gateway enables private instances to initiate outbound IPv4 communication while preventing unsolicited inbound connections through the NAT service.</p><div class="note"><strong>Production design:</strong> Put a NAT Gateway in each AZ and keep each private subnet routed to its same-AZ NAT Gateway to reduce cross-AZ dependencies. For AWS service access, evaluate private VPC endpoints and endpoint policies to reduce unnecessary NAT traffic.</div></section>
<section class="section" id="security"><h2><i class="bi bi-shield-lock me-2"></i>05 · Security and resilience controls</h2><div class="row g-3"><div class="col-lg-6"><h3>Security groups — stateful</h3><ul><li>ALB: inbound TCP 443 from authorized clients.</li><li>App tier: application port only from ALB security group.</li><li>Database: database port only from application security group.</li><li>Apply least-privilege egress and log relevant network flows.</li></ul></div><div class="col-lg-6"><h3>Network ACLs — stateless</h3><ul><li>Provide subnet-boundary allow/deny rules.</li><li>Explicitly consider return and ephemeral port ranges.</li><li>Use security groups for fine-grained workload policy.</li><li>Monitor with VPC Flow Logs and CloudWatch.</li></ul></div></div><p>Multi-AZ placement improves availability but does not automatically make an application highly available. Configure load balancer health checks, Auto Scaling / ECS capacity spread, database resilience and backups, and test failure scenarios.</p></section>
<section class="section" id="lab"><h2><i class="bi bi-tools me-2"></i>06 · Hands-on exercise: build the network in AWS</h2><div class="step"><strong>Step 1 — Create the VPC.</strong><div>Open AWS VPC Console → Your VPCs → Create VPC. Select IPv4 CIDR <code>10.20.0.0/16</code>, enable DNS support and DNS hostnames as needed.</div></div><div class="step"><strong>Step 2 — Create six subnets.</strong><div>Use the six CIDR blocks shown above, with three in AZ A and three in AZ B.</div></div><div class="step"><strong>Step 3 — Configure public routing.</strong><div>Create and attach an Internet Gateway, create a public route table with <code>0.0.0.0/0 → IGW</code>, and associate both public subnets.</div></div><div class="step"><strong>Step 4 — Configure NAT and private routing.</strong><div>Create one NAT Gateway in each public subnet with its own Elastic IP. Configure one private-app route table per AZ and route default IPv4 traffic to the same-AZ NAT.</div></div><div class="step"><strong>Step 5 — Configure isolation.</strong><div>Associate isolated database subnets with route tables that have no default Internet route. Create a DB subnet group containing both database subnets.</div></div><div class="step"><strong>Step 6 — Deploy and secure workloads.</strong><div>Deploy an internet-facing ALB into both public subnets and app targets into private subnets; establish least-privilege security group references.</div></div><div class="step"><strong>Step 7 — Verify.</strong><div>Validate ingress through ALB, private-app egress through NAT, denial of direct Internet access to private workloads, and database tier restrictions. Review route tables, target health and VPC Flow Logs.</div></div><div class="note"><strong>Cost caution:</strong> NAT Gateways, load balancing, public IPv4 addresses, compute and RDS resources may incur charges even in demonstrations. Tear down unused lab resources when finished.</div></section>
<section class="section"><h2><i class="bi bi-check2-square me-2"></i>Knowledge check</h2><ol><li>How many total and AWS-usable IPv4 addresses does a <code>/24</code> subnet provide?</li><li>Why should private application subnets use a NAT Gateway instead of routing directly through an Internet Gateway?</li><li>Why does each Availability Zone have a separate public, app and database subnet?</li><li>Can a database subnet be considered isolated if it has a default route to a NAT Gateway?</li><li>What is the practical difference between stateful security groups and stateless network ACLs?</li></ol></section></main><footer class="footer">Commercial IT Bootcamp · AWS Architecture Learning Series · Illustrative architecture for education</footer><script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/bootstrap@5.3.3/dist/js/bootstrap.bundle.min.js"></script></body></html>
